[{:db/id "q-140383/selection/052f33f71e52f4a0c5dde0c5bad145eace99239583a9a6a96e472f9876753fb3" :answer/id #uuid "494ad08e-b220-5b32-bd03-23643745c2ce" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\lambda =4-3\\text{i}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140383/selection/ba7923c9f51f35e5772889395dbe185effec82a49386902db0e31bbf3d672b3f" :answer/id #uuid "0cbb9d12-4ae9-5a48-a17b-3a75f45dcfe8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\lambda =4+3\\text{i}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140383/selection/6941cff48cebd9e4c9822110bc53fcdb7790ba04f618b331a0a8be09ff036b06" :answer/id #uuid "8189ed53-deac-56bc-8250-a321f067f001" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$v$ is not an eigenvector of $A$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140383/selection/e3b2bf0cdad4de4205941520b04ee9e69465027639973814abbc16421feb0f26" :answer/id #uuid "3cadc242-f649-541a-b484-089d5bdd4141" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\lambda =3+4\\text{i}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140383/selection/571ca2f77b31ca306f9dc4183afea70c17a1c40ab756f9dcdf59a623e25a605b" :answer/id #uuid "ee79e0e0-6a8c-502c-ac27-a4913d40a7b1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\lambda =3-4\\text{i}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140383/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "fd077df7-8220-5b57-82cf-62aead5b0794" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-140383/selection/ba7923c9f51f35e5772889395dbe185effec82a49386902db0e31bbf3d672b3f" :answer-field/choices ["q-140383/selection/052f33f71e52f4a0c5dde0c5bad145eace99239583a9a6a96e472f9876753fb3" "q-140383/selection/ba7923c9f51f35e5772889395dbe185effec82a49386902db0e31bbf3d672b3f" "q-140383/selection/6941cff48cebd9e4c9822110bc53fcdb7790ba04f618b331a0a8be09ff036b06" "q-140383/selection/e3b2bf0cdad4de4205941520b04ee9e69465027639973814abbc16421feb0f26" "q-140383/selection/571ca2f77b31ca306f9dc4183afea70c17a1c40ab756f9dcdf59a623e25a605b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-140383" :question/id #uuid "cfeca104-fe47-5c0d-8612-60fbd83f3f78" :question/math-academy-id "q-140383" :question/problem "Determine if $v=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i} \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}].$\nIf so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?" :question/worked-solution "The eigenvectors are non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\text{i} \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3+4\\text{i} \\\\ -4-3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3{\\text{i}}^{2}+4\\text{i} \\\\ -4-3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i}(4+3\\text{i}) \\\\ (-1)(4+3\\text{i})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =(4+3\\text{i})[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i} \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =(4+3\\text{i})v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Av=(4+3\\text{i})v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =4+3\\text{i}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-140383/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d0e83dcf-3ce6-5583-bd28-fdbc805a9403"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-140383"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-140384/selection/59d250c13181175618e3be9db81c0ec52481f1ca0408fd05e452d847aa95ffb9" :answer/id #uuid "c16cf0e8-24d1-5fd8-82bd-09e4bc7de781" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\lambda =3-2\\text{i}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140384/selection/dc5a074b6d91e10f5b6820eae01228e6c6c181d97b24fc4636c61b7d9f5c9fa0" :answer/id #uuid "cf87e6c2-102f-51a9-88df-2b42a0d33172" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\lambda =2+3\\text{i}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140384/selection/6941cff48cebd9e4c9822110bc53fcdb7790ba04f618b331a0a8be09ff036b06" :answer/id #uuid "ee0e595d-bda3-5307-9825-4fbe80444ba4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$v$ is not an eigenvector of $A$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140384/selection/ec666d1dd1a1ce5246fdb75e089babb7bfb1bc7bb76b75946c4204a0bde9019b" :answer/id #uuid "f02cf6b9-7107-5597-b97b-8a9b67442511" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\lambda =2-3\\text{i}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140384/selection/80a66eaaafeafd27b1afe0129a33daf6d0618e500892baf48ebd69f2ba0d8b39" :answer/id #uuid "5b1032ae-603d-5980-b664-73e9368ee165" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\lambda =3+2\\text{i}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140384/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "dd0b0847-d6de-549b-ae6c-60ea1caec4e5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-140384/selection/ec666d1dd1a1ce5246fdb75e089babb7bfb1bc7bb76b75946c4204a0bde9019b" :answer-field/choices ["q-140384/selection/59d250c13181175618e3be9db81c0ec52481f1ca0408fd05e452d847aa95ffb9" "q-140384/selection/dc5a074b6d91e10f5b6820eae01228e6c6c181d97b24fc4636c61b7d9f5c9fa0" "q-140384/selection/6941cff48cebd9e4c9822110bc53fcdb7790ba04f618b331a0a8be09ff036b06" "q-140384/selection/ec666d1dd1a1ce5246fdb75e089babb7bfb1bc7bb76b75946c4204a0bde9019b" "q-140384/selection/80a66eaaafeafd27b1afe0129a33daf6d0618e500892baf48ebd69f2ba0d8b39"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-140384" :question/id #uuid "2a27e7a9-e009-56bc-8df0-b8ef241b7ee6" :question/math-academy-id "q-140384" :question/problem "Determine whether $v=[\\begin{aligned}3\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 9 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}].$\nIf so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?" :question/worked-solution "The eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 9 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}9+6\\text{i} \\\\ 2-3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-9{\\text{i}}^{2}+6\\text{i} \\\\ 2-3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3\\text{i}(2-3\\text{i}) \\\\ 1(2-3\\text{i})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =(2-3\\text{i})[\\begin{aligned}3\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =(2-3\\text{i})v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Av=(2-3\\text{i})v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =2-3\\text{i}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-140384/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d0e83dcf-3ce6-5583-bd28-fdbc805a9403"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-140384"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-39185"] :question/problem "If $v=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}10 & 4 \\\\ -26 & -10\\end{aligned}]$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2\\text{i},$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{b}{a}?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =-2\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}10 & 4 \\\\ -26 & -10\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-2\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}10+2\\text{i} & 4 \\\\ -26 & -10+2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(10+2\\text{i}){x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -26{x}_{1}+(-10+2\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A+2\\text{i}I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables to be the free variable. For example, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}(5+\\text{i}){x}_{1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}(5+\\text{i}){x}_{1}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{1}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=-2,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 5+\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{5+\\text{i}}{-2}=-\\frac{5}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\text{i}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-140386/selection/2b039b330a9588d1b1119eb85dfaea42c8ed4cf4efcfe203b599538af370bdc5" :answer/id #uuid "d6ffd505-0aca-55df-b883-063397d6cc1d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}-\\frac{1}{5}\\text{i}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140386/selection/f65f99457b3b74a0c153e8669a5302df1953562863c9e842712e3949efe5081e" :answer/id #uuid "404b51f2-4d65-579b-8196-889dc878b3f5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{5}+\\frac{1}{5}\\text{i}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140386/selection/25844af39a8d2d3e15e22b7dec3095cea1c2f5e1add5c6b31e9f69384aefadac" :answer/id #uuid "d9fa9dc7-1985-50e8-ba92-8c4f0c74195a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}+\\frac{1}{5}\\text{i}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140386/selection/af1f7129f754baf91c6e69f772c316f8bb75767f93d2c68afa4781b738aca8d1" :answer/id #uuid "2a7405f3-c68a-5000-b8c2-43c6e21a5bdb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\text{i}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140386/selection/9ea35b384437c1918f8bae1b8480dda6ce817bbc9dd3b9002d350db945f5a776" :answer/id #uuid "ae094918-bda3-50d6-8989-ac52320024ef" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\text{i}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140386/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "d491997a-9175-5259-809d-b95e13173eb1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-140386/selection/f65f99457b3b74a0c153e8669a5302df1953562863c9e842712e3949efe5081e" :answer-field/choices ["q-140386/selection/2b039b330a9588d1b1119eb85dfaea42c8ed4cf4efcfe203b599538af370bdc5" "q-140386/selection/f65f99457b3b74a0c153e8669a5302df1953562863c9e842712e3949efe5081e" "q-140386/selection/25844af39a8d2d3e15e22b7dec3095cea1c2f5e1add5c6b31e9f69384aefadac" "q-140386/selection/af1f7129f754baf91c6e69f772c316f8bb75767f93d2c68afa4781b738aca8d1" "q-140386/selection/9ea35b384437c1918f8bae1b8480dda6ce817bbc9dd3b9002d350db945f5a776"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-140386" :question/id #uuid "01a38607-b587-5299-b88e-717f9d67686a" :question/math-academy-id "q-140386" :question/problem "If $v=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -5 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-\\text{i},$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{b}{a}?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =-\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -5 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3+\\text{i} & -5 \\\\ 2 & 3+\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(-3+\\text{i}){x}_{1}-5{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+(3+\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A+\\text{i}I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables to be the free variable. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{5}(-3+\\text{i}){x}_{1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ \\frac{1}{5}(-3+\\text{i}){x}_{1}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{1}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=5,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -3+\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{-3+\\text{i}}{5}=-\\frac{3}{5}+\\frac{1}{5}\\text{i}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-140386/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "00cf6180-070b-5ebf-af24-bc4c4b0bd94e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-140386"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-81190/selection/56019ff80d11d678dbd5eca83c729f420f1b6a8ad4259032846618d6b48aa0bc" :answer/id #uuid "6e8805f9-1c6e-521c-a98a-cc0f79fe6a50" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-81190/selection/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" :answer/id #uuid "b36e4cb5-c904-56fb-a24e-dd76af5a2e67" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-81190/selection/4280bcd0914e40dbdc039bc97ee5d5baafd2654e75e9b692316d9c28c4842856" :answer/id #uuid "1fffe5aa-ee8a-5b26-8ade-c9c24234c182" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-81190/selection/a292c43a5bdd99f5ad91dc2c1ffcf1365e0f66bcc30237684298c4592b5a76aa" :answer/id #uuid "b212765d-6088-51dd-bcb2-b84794cb59e1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3\\text{i}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-81190/selection/768cf74dca4dd813660bb6962d5a6b89b880fe3634b6cfb9334c3b6a497a15f3" :answer/id #uuid "bf6a9dad-8386-5818-983d-4906ea5a545f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\text{i}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-81190/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "2d6d9648-4bd5-5a09-8c96-d66b096cb920" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-81190/selection/4280bcd0914e40dbdc039bc97ee5d5baafd2654e75e9b692316d9c28c4842856" :answer-field/choices ["q-81190/selection/56019ff80d11d678dbd5eca83c729f420f1b6a8ad4259032846618d6b48aa0bc" "q-81190/selection/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" "q-81190/selection/4280bcd0914e40dbdc039bc97ee5d5baafd2654e75e9b692316d9c28c4842856" "q-81190/selection/a292c43a5bdd99f5ad91dc2c1ffcf1365e0f66bcc30237684298c4592b5a76aa" "q-81190/selection/768cf74dca4dd813660bb6962d5a6b89b880fe3634b6cfb9334c3b6a497a15f3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-81190" :question/id #uuid "defa1cb1-365b-5e5f-ab67-ca3d065551d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-81190" :question/problem "Let ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{1}\\end{aligned}]$ and ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ be linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 5 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}].$\nGiven that ${\\lambda }_{1}=3\\text{i}$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ find the value of $\\frac{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,{\\lambda }_{1}=3\\text{i}$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{1}I & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 5 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}3\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1-3\\text{i} & 5 \\\\ -2 & 1-3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(-1-3\\text{i}){x}_{1}+5{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+(1-3\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-3\\text{i}I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as the free variable. For example, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{5}(1+3\\text{i}){x}_{1}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ \\frac{1}{5}(1+3\\text{i}){x}_{1}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{1}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=5,$ we get the eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1+3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nOn the other hand, since ${\\lambda }_{1}=3\\text{i}$ is a complex eigenvalue of $A,$ the second eigenvalue of $A$ must be the  complex conjugate of ${\\lambda }_{1}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{2}=\\overset{―}{{\\lambda }_{1}}=-3\\text{i}.\n$$\n\nMoreover, the corresponding eigenvector to ${\\lambda }_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1-3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}=\\frac{(5)(5)}{(1+3\\text{i})(1-3\\text{i})}=\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-81190/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ad17d11-325f-5888-a5cd-61ee27f7a103"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-81190"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-39187/selection/c7e2c99672184a6ffc5fe21255ac37862a4c27e4b225c7b645ba47d8f3c76eda" :answer/id #uuid "595fdd39-4f69-5e94-be96-018923aa641d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-39187/selection/3f800cbbb08b479cb563447eed24c12fb8f864d66caa24893560453c756cbcb1" :answer/id #uuid "e8d01964-7f9e-59fa-aa7e-073f5ce56aea" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{25}{29}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-39187/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "8f3fcf41-b2f5-59d7-af80-98ca2e37a9e5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-39187/selection/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" :answer/id #uuid "47502250-b107-585d-906b-4b32c34377fa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-39187/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "86b113d6-a6a1-5ec7-88b2-0d4d15cb2999" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-39187/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "a2f35160-927e-5f50-9e29-b16c734a2eca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-39187/selection/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" :answer-field/choices ["q-39187/selection/c7e2c99672184a6ffc5fe21255ac37862a4c27e4b225c7b645ba47d8f3c76eda" "q-39187/selection/3f800cbbb08b479cb563447eed24c12fb8f864d66caa24893560453c756cbcb1" "q-39187/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" "q-39187/selection/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" "q-39187/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-39187" :question/id #uuid "6ce55420-8f30-5a2d-834c-83df9e2c32db" :question/math-academy-id "q-39187" :question/problem "Let ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{1}\\end{aligned}]$ and ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ be linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 3 \\\\ -15 & -5\\end{aligned}].$\nGiven that ${\\lambda }_{1}=1-3\\text{i}$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ find the value of $\\frac{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,{\\lambda }_{1}=1-3\\text{i}$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{1}I & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & 3 \\\\ -15 & -5\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1-3\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1-3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6+3\\text{i} & 3 \\\\ -15 & -6+3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(6+3\\text{i}){x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -15{x}_{1}+(-6+3\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(1-3\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as the free variable. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{5}(-2+\\text{i}){x}_{2}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5}(-2+\\text{i}){x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=5,$ we get the eigenvector\n\n$$\n{\\text{v}}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2+\\text{i} \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nOn the other hand, since ${\\lambda }_{1}=1-3\\text{i}$ is a complex eigenvalue of $A,$ the second eigenvalue of $A$ must be the  complex conjugate of ${\\lambda }_{1}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{2}=\\overset{―}{{\\lambda }_{1}}=1+3\\text{i}.\n$$\n\nMoreover, the corresponding eigenvector to ${\\lambda }_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2-\\text{i} \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}=\\frac{(5)(5)}{(-2+\\text{i})(-2-\\text{i})}=5.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-39187/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ad17d11-325f-5888-a5cd-61ee27f7a103"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-39187"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-39188/selection/a8dd3023a15736f67ea6d8ea5b4152008e8f88c3ce9ec6730573f2f45df5d928" :answer/id #uuid "90c77030-9cf4-5578-966b-acfc2fa9adec" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{29}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-39188/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "a7b85246-0ab6-5181-9632-508cce9a36e8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-39188/selection/c7e2c99672184a6ffc5fe21255ac37862a4c27e4b225c7b645ba47d8f3c76eda" :answer/id #uuid "7ddd3b59-8223-5bf0-bd2a-3e8718ebea03" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-39188/selection/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" :answer/id #uuid "25535043-dea9-5459-998d-edcb9baf3623" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-39188/selection/ebe3072a1dd0240f82f7c04e06603ef971401ed1aea6a421c38664ef5adb09f3" :answer/id #uuid "46c68393-3e48-5ce6-8018-cc90f4b27a1f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "29" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-39188/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "14560430-1af1-5053-a3fd-ec6331811b91" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-39188/selection/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" :answer-field/choices ["q-39188/selection/a8dd3023a15736f67ea6d8ea5b4152008e8f88c3ce9ec6730573f2f45df5d928" "q-39188/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" "q-39188/selection/c7e2c99672184a6ffc5fe21255ac37862a4c27e4b225c7b645ba47d8f3c76eda" "q-39188/selection/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" "q-39188/selection/ebe3072a1dd0240f82f7c04e06603ef971401ed1aea6a421c38664ef5adb09f3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-39188" :question/id #uuid "62f097b5-cd5d-5f7e-878a-f8d5552fa6c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-39188" :question/problem "Let ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{1}\\end{aligned}]$ and ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ be linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-8 & -4 \\\\ 20 & 8\\end{aligned}].$\nGiven that ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4\\text{i}$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ find the value of $\\frac{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,{\\lambda }_{1}=-4\\text{i}$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{1}I & =[\\begin{aligned}-8 & -4 \\\\ 20 & 8\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-4\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-8+4\\text{i} & -4 \\\\ 20 & 8+4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(-8+4\\text{i}){x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 20{x}_{1}+(8+4\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(-4\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as the free variable. For example, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=(-2+\\text{i}){x}_{1}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ (-2+\\text{i}){x}_{1}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{1}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=1,$ we get the eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2+\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nOn the other hand, since ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4\\text{i}$ is a complex eigenvalue of $A,$ the second eigenvalue of $A$ must be the  complex conjugate of ${\\lambda }_{1}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{2}=\\overset{―}{{\\lambda }_{1}}=4\\text{i}.\n$$\n\nMoreover, the corresponding eigenvector to ${\\lambda }_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2-\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}=\\frac{(-2+\\text{i})(-2-\\text{i})}{(1)(1)}=5.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-39188/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ad17d11-325f-5888-a5cd-61ee27f7a103"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-39188"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5528"] :question/problem "Determine whether ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/assets/59af73d1cda1a988c775ccf198b53653b96660b4ad8416add1b0c1816e89a5a5.png) is an eigenvector of the matrix ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/assets/963bcaf94bc481fe8fbc99ca28fba037047d8821661eadcf0e5e9ce50ba39ef8.png)\nIf so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5527"] :question/problem "Find an eigenvector of \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/assets/6e4a720878567b81cb593aa5a9bfc5aa2c8c7ace61d111bb8934bc94056c04bc.png)\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=-3+4\\textrm{i}.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I) \\textbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =-3+4\\textrm{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 4 \\\\ -4 & -3\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-3+4\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3+4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4\\text{i} & 4 \\\\ -4 & -4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-4\\text{i}{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -4{x}_{1}-4\\text{i}{x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(-3+4\\textrm{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another.\n\nAs a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables to be the free variable. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\nx_1 = -\\textrm{i}x_2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/assets/0d2e7b9f7011624cf74762419efd784144cf41e2000301d093e1aa612e96f495.png)\n\nSetting $x_2=1,$ we get the eigenvector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/assets/2e4bc467e9fbc15574d2739766eada3f297500ecf1a69382500271751b2edf65.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5529"] :question/problem "Let ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/assets/07f01e2a202e29fee28a98d386df1d22fd21efcf1ee4d0a6a4cc56f9b637d57c.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/assets/a547975b3524322daec753a8ddd010e50e815307d72bc07933d1e3674b4b462c.png) be linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/assets/53de45d6b1504ec53ca81471731b06ac5524a12230f0414553624c116a6f47c6.png)\nGiven that $\\lambda_1=4+5\\textrm{i}$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ find the value of ${\\dfrac{a_1a_2}{b_1b_2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I) \\textbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda_1 =4+5\\textrm{i}$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{1}I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ -5 & 4\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}4+5\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4+5\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5\\text{i} & 5 \\\\ -5 & -5\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-5\\text{i}{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -5{x}_{1}-5\\text{i}{x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(4+5\\textrm{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another.\n\nAs a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as the free variable. For example, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx_2 = \\textrm{i}x_1.\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is given by\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/assets/b212192b142e9b4686a4d39e5957db5f3bfbca6283bfcb1d88a6fc57a4a27848.png)\n\nSetting $x_1=1,$ we get the eigenvector\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/assets/65c073a60a27234349281e873958c2c9d54fb703331a4de23cc358a3eab1b232.png) \n\nNow, since $\\lambda_1=4+5\\textrm{i}$ is a complex eigenvalue of $A,$ the second eigenvalue of $A$ must be the complex conjugate of $\\lambda_1.$ Therefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\lambda_2=\\overline{\\lambda_1}=4-5\\textrm{i}.\n$$\n\nMoreover, the corresponding eigenvector must be the complex conjugate of $\\mathbf v_1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/assets/ca11ae0cca8d07c026108e71b96398171597f7330cb2390d871a8071e7bba873.png)\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\dfrac{a_1a_2}{b_1b_2}} = {\\dfrac{(1)(1)}{(\\textrm{i})(-\\textrm{i})}} = 1.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
